Nicole:

I'm afraid it is not quite as simple as that.  You'll note that the line
of code that you wish to change (the one that creates object dsm.44)
make a call to the function dsm.fit().  If you have a look at that
function, you will see that function in turn calls the function gam()
from the mgcv package (because the argument 'fn' requests the fit of a gam).

If you explore the R documentation for gam() in mgcv, you'll be directed
to the description of 'family' describing how you specify the family of
the error structure for your model.  Tweedie is not one of the choices
available to you in the 'family' argument to gam().

Instead you will need to investigate how to fit your model not using the
dsm.fit() function, but rather working from the constituent R objects
(sample.dat.44 and obs.dat.44) you will build the structure necessary to
fit your model with the error structure you desire.  I've not worked
with fitting models with the Tweedie error structure, so I can't
describe how it does work.

